{
  "id": "80846",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Unit Key Equations and Key Terms",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "4t8ptenj8jj3k6g",
                "text": "Key Equations"
              }
            ],
            "id": "3564456627",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Coming soon"
              }
            ],
            "id": "f0z6foaxjwd8d4d",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "7wj13h8aak0ltne",
                "text": "Key Terms"
              }
            ],
            "id": "32spi6arvy7feyb",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "actinide series"
              },
              {
                "text": " Actinium and the elements in the second row or the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "f"
              },
              {
                "text": "-block, atomic numbers 89–103. Also called "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "actinoid series"
              },
              {
                "text": "."
              }
            ],
            "id": "846482101",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "bidentate ligand"
              },
              {
                "text": " A ligand that coordinates to one central metal through coordinate bonds from two different atoms."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3411727686",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "central metal"
              },
              {
                "text": " An ion or atom to which one or more ligands is attached through coordinate covalent bonds."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3715516470",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "chelate"
              },
              {
                "text": " A complex formed from a polydentate ligand attached to a central metal."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2390656415",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "chelating ligand"
              },
              {
                "text": " A ligand that attaches to a central metal ion by bonds from two or more donor atoms."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1553070188",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "em": true,
                "term": true,
                "text": "cis"
              },
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": " configuration"
              },
              {
                "text": " Configuration of a geometrical isomer in which two similar groups are on the same side of an imaginary reference line on the molecule."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1765409385",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "coordinate covalent bond"
              },
              {
                "text": " A covalent bond in which both electrons originated from the same atom; also called dative bond."
              }
            ],
            "id": "420607137",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "coordination compound"
              },
              {
                "text": " A stable substance consisting of a central metal atom or ion (acting as a Lewis acid) surrounded by molecules or ions (ligands) that donate pairs of electrons (acting as Lewis bases) through coordinate covalent bonds."
              }
            ],
            "id": "511511309",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "coordination number"
              },
              {
                "text": " The number of coordinate covalent bonds to the central metal atom in a complex or the number of closest contacts to an atom in a crystalline form."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2034557715",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "coordination sphere"
              },
              {
                "text": " The central metal atom or ion plus the attached ligands of a complex."
              }
            ],
            "id": "631630459",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "counterion"
              },
              {
                "text": " An ion of opposite charge that accompanies a charged species in a compound to maintain electrical neutrality; it balances the charge of complex ions within a coordination compound."
              }
            ],
            "id": "334849411",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "crystal field splitting (Δ)"
              },
              {
                "text": " Splitting the energies of the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals due to differences in their interactions with ligands."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2019217422",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "crystal field theory"
              },
              {
                "text": " A model that explains the energies of the orbitals in transition metals in terms of electrostatic interactions with the ligands but does not include metal ligand bonding."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3307395284",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "em": true,
                "term": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "-block elements"
              },
              {
                "text": " Elements in groups 3 through 11 with valence electrons in "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3333217862",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "denticity"
              },
              {
                "text": " The number of donor atoms in a ligand that directly bind to a central metal atom or ion in a coordination complex. It indicates how many points of attachment the ligand has to the metal (monodentate, bidentate, etc.) based on the number of coordinating atoms."
              }
            ],
            "id": "108470879",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "diamagnetic"
              },
              {
                "text": " Describes substances that are repelled by an external magnetic field due to the absence of unpaired electrons. Diamagnetic materials create an induced magnetic field in a direction opposite to the applied magnetic field and do not have a net magnetic moment."
              }
            ],
            "id": "429520045",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "donor atom"
              },
              {
                "text": " An atom in a ligand with a lone pair of electrons that forms a coordinate covalent bond to a central metal."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2796910470",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "electrolysis"
              },
              {
                "text": " A process using electrical energy to cause a nonspontaneous process to occur."
              }
            ],
            "id": "4137533716",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "em": true,
                "term": true,
                "text": "f"
              },
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "-block elements"
              },
              {
                "text": " Elements with atomic numbers 58–71 or 90–103 that have valence electrons in "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "f"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals; they are frequently shown offset below the periodic table. Also called "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "inner transition elements"
              },
              {
                "text": "."
              }
            ],
            "id": "4263863620",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "first transition series"
              },
              {
                "text": " Transition elements in the fourth period of the periodic table (first row of the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": "-block), atomic numbers 21–30."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3252605280",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "fourth transition series"
              },
              {
                "text": " Transition elements in the seventh period of the periodic table (fourth row of the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": "-block), atomic numbers 89 and 104–111."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3314615337",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "geometric isomers"
              },
              {
                "text": " Isomers that differ in the way in which atoms are oriented in space relative to each other, leading to different physical and chemical properties."
              }
            ],
            "id": "4050989852",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "high-spin complex"
              },
              {
                "text": " A complex in which the electrons maximize the total electron spin by singly populating all of the orbitals before pairing two electrons into the lower-energy orbitals."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1197903818",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "Hund's rule"
              },
              {
                "text": " Every orbital in a subshell is singly occupied with one electron before any one orbital is doubly occupied, and all electrons in singly occupied orbitals have the same spin."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1492680164",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "ionization isomer"
              },
              {
                "text": " An isomer in which a ligand has exchanged places with an anion or molecule that was originally outside the coordination sphere."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2204782526",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "lanthanide series"
              },
              {
                "text": " Lanthanum and the elements in the first row or the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "f"
              },
              {
                "text": "-block, atomic numbers 57–71. Also called "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "lanthanoid series"
              },
              {
                "text": "."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3651583403",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "ligand"
              },
              {
                "text": " An ion or a neutral molecule attached to the central metal ion in a coordination compound."
              }
            ],
            "id": "315117371",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "linkage isomer"
              },
              {
                "text": " Coordination compound with the same composition but with a ligand that can bind to the transition metal in different ways (such as CN"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": " vs. NC"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": ")."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1985072501",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "low-spin complex"
              },
              {
                "text": " A complex in which the electrons minimize the total electron spin by pairing in the lower-energy orbitals before populating the higher-energy orbitals."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2427218250",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "magnetic moment"
              },
              {
                "text": " A measure of the strength and direction of the magnetic field produced by a magnet or a current-carrying loop of wire. In the context of atoms or molecules, it is the measure of the net magnetic field resulting from the spin and orbital angular momentum of electrons. The magnetic moment determines the torque a magnet will experience in an external magnetic field."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3357559515",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "monodentate ligand"
              },
              {
                "text": " A ligand that attaches to a central metal through just one coordinate covalent bond."
              }
            ],
            "id": "769944444",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "octet rule"
              },
              {
                "text": " The tendency of main-group elements to form structures in which each atom has 8 valence electrons."
              }
            ],
            "id": "693210554",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "optical isomer"
              },
              {
                "text": " A molecule that is a nonsuperimposable mirror image with identical chemical and physical properties, except when it reacts with other optical isomers. Also called "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "enantiomer"
              },
              {
                "text": "."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1403990303",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "pairing energy (P)"
              },
              {
                "text": " Energy required to place two electrons with opposite spins into a single orbital."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3977785017",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "paramagnetic"
              },
              {
                "text": " Describes substances that are attracted to an external magnetic field due to the presence of unpaired electrons. Paramagnetic materials have a net magnetic moment and are weakly magnetized in the direction of the magnetic field."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3449286140",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "polydentate ligand"
              },
              {
                "text": " A ligand that is attached to a central metal ion by bonds from two or more donor atoms, named with prefixes specifying how many donors are present (e.g., hexadentate = six coordinate bonds formed)."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2659170603",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "reducing agent"
              },
              {
                "text": " A substance that brings about the reduction of another substance, and in the process becomes oxidized. Also called "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "reductant"
              },
              {
                "text": "."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2153146371",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "reduction"
              },
              {
                "text": " A process in which a reactant gains one or more electrons resulting in a decrease in oxidation number."
              }
            ],
            "id": "382000355",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "second transition series"
              },
              {
                "text": " Transition elements in the fifth period of the periodic table (second row of the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": "-block), atomic numbers 39–48."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1565625235",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "smelting"
              },
              {
                "text": " The process of extracting a pure metal from a molten ore."
              }
            ],
            "id": "159239047",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "spectrochemical series"
              },
              {
                "text": " A ranking of ligands according to the magnitude of the crystal field splitting they induce."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2053630230",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "strong-field ligands"
              },
              {
                "text": " Ligand that cause larger crystal field splittings."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1287405572",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "third transition series"
              },
              {
                "text": " Transition elements in the sixth period of the periodic table (third row of the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": "-block), atomic numbers 57 and 72–80."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3508238996",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "em": true,
                "term": true,
                "text": "trans"
              },
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": " configuration"
              },
              {
                "text": " Configuration of a geometrical isomer in which two similar groups are on opposite sides of an imaginary reference line on the molecule."
              }
            ],
            "id": "282350965",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "transition metals"
              },
              {
                "text": " Elements that have (or that readily form) partially filled "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1385501307",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "weak-field ligands"
              },
              {
                "text": " Ligands that cause small crystal field splittings."
              }
            ],
            "id": "77j56vfx1uh6cyr",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "editor": "slate",
